    "title": "$1 million scratch-off ticket sold near Cleveland as Powerball drawing nears - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Ann Lipsett ,\n8 hours ago\nCLEVELAND (WJW) – A lucky lottery player purchased a $1 million ticket in Northeast Ohio.\nThe winning ticket was sold at a Village Gas Station in Milan according to a media release from the Ohio Lottery .\nPowerball jackpot hits $1 billion for Wednesday The winning $30 Wild Cash Tripler Scratch-Off awarded the player a prize of $40K a year for 25 years. The whopping $1 million prize was claimed by The Windfall Fairfield Trust, represented by Joseph M. Nixon, Esq.\nInstead of the $1 million annuity, the trust elected to take a one-time cash payment of $500,000. After mandatory 26.75% federal and Ohio state taxes, the trust will receive $366,250.\nSenate passes bill that could change nickels and how you pay with cash Lottery fever isn’t just hitting the Buckeye State. Wednesday night’s Powerball drawing has climbed to over $1 billion, making it the largest Powerball jackpot so far this year.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to Fox 8 Cleveland WJW.\n8",
    "summary": "CLEVELAND (WJW) – A lucky lottery player purchased a $1 million ticket in Northeast Ohio. The winning ticket was sold at a Village Gas Station in Milan according to a media release from the Ohio Lottery . The whopping $1 million prize was claimed by The Windfall Fairfield Trust, represented by Joseph M. Nixon, Esq. Senate passes bill that could change nickels and how you pay with cash Lottery fever isn’t just hitting the Buckeye State. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to Fox 8 Cleveland WJW.",
